  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cam Schlittler turned a tied game into a one-run masterpiece, and the offense found the door in the tenth.",
    "what_this_means": "Schlittler did what the best starters do in close games. He left no room for error. Eight innings, one run, nine strikeouts. The Angels matched that discipline early, and through six innings the game was locked at 1-1, neither team willing to break first. But late-inning rallies have become this team's signature. Austin Wells broke the tie in the tenth with a single that felt inevitable the moment he stepped in. One swing turned a 1-1 slog into a 6-3 finish. The bullpen didn't have to rescue anything. Bednar and Fulmer closed it clean, three strikeouts across two innings. This is what happens when the starter does everything right and the offense finally answers: the margin doesn't waver.",
    "what_to_watch": "San Diego starts Walker Buehler tonight with a 4.66 ERA. Max Fried has allowed two runs or fewer in each of his last four starts.",
